// What a read answered.
//
// **Here, and not in `packages/platform`, because of who has to name the type.** The streaming
// transforms in `gzip` and `stream` take the source as a funcref — `gzipStream(cli.readChunk, …)` —
// and wac has no closures, so no adapter can sit between them: whatever `readChunk` answers is
// exactly what those signatures must accept. Putting `Read` in `platform` would therefore have made
// two pure algorithm packages depend on the capability world for a data shape. `bytes` is the lowest
// package in the tree and already the home of `Buf`, so both sides can reach it and neither gains a
// dependency it should not have.
//
// The shape itself exists because the obvious answer, `u8[]`, has a hole in it: an empty array means
// both "the input finished" and "the read failed". Every filter in this repo treated the second as
// the first, so a copy from a dying disk exited 0 having written half its output, and `gzipStream`
// produced a *valid* archive of half the input with a CRC to match. The first attempt at a fix was a
// companion `inputError()` to ask afterwards — which left the ordinary path looking exactly as
// correct as it had been, so anyone who forgot to ask got the old behaviour back. This cannot be
// forgotten: `match` is exhaustive, and a caller that ignores `Failed` does not compile.

export enum Read {
  /** Bytes, and never zero of them — an empty answer is `End`. */
  Data(u8[] bytes),
  /** The input finished. Not a failure. */
  End,
  /** It did not finish, and this is the host's own message. */
  Failed(string why)
}
